---
import ToolPage from "../components/ToolPage.astro";
import StampTool from "../tools/StampTool";
---

<ToolPage tool="stamp">
    <Fragment slot="lead">
        Swap filled and empty: every area your design covers comes out <strong class="text-slate-300">untouched</strong>,
        and everything around it comes out <strong class="text-slate-300">engraved away</strong> — so the artwork
        stands proud of the plate. That is what a rubber stamp needs; the base plate and the handle to go with it
        come off the same page. It makes stencils, inlays and any "engrave the background" job too.
    </Fragment>

    <StampTool client:only="react">
        <div slot="fallback" class="flex h-[100dvh] flex-col bg-[#08090d]">
            <div class="h-12 shrink-0 border-b border-white/7"></div>
            <div class="flex min-h-0 flex-1">
                <div class="hidden w-60 shrink-0 border-r border-white/7 xl:block"></div>
                <div class="flex-1"></div>
                <div class="hidden w-76 shrink-0 border-l border-white/7 xl:block"></div>
            </div>
            <div class="h-7 shrink-0 border-t border-white/7"></div>
        </div>
    </StampTool>

    <section slot="notes" class="relative px-4 pb-24">
        <div class="mx-auto w-[min(72rem,100%)]">
            <div class="grid gap-5 lg:grid-cols-4">
                <article class="glass reveal rounded-2xl p-6">
                    <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">Exact, not re-traced</h2>
                    <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                        A design's filled area is already defined by nesting: an outermost ring is filled, a ring
                        inside it is a hole, a ring inside that is filled again — the even-odd rule. So one more
                        ring <em>around</em> everything shifts every region up a level and flips it. No bitmap, no
                        resampling: the result is accurate to the 0.01 mm the curves were flattened to.
                    </p>
                </article>
                <article class="glass reveal rounded-2xl p-6" style="--reveal-delay: 110ms">
                    <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">Mirror it for a stamp</h2>
                    <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                        A stamp prints what touches the paper, so it has to be engraved back-to-front or the text
                        comes out reversed. Set <strong class="text-slate-300">Orientation</strong> to <strong class="text-slate-300">Mirror ↔</strong> and the design
                        flips about its own centre — the plate stays exactly where it was.
                    </p>
                </article>
                <article class="glass reveal rounded-2xl p-6" style="--reveal-delay: 220ms">
                    <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">Name the size</h2>
                    <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                        The stamp opens at a size you name — <strong class="text-slate-300">Set the size</strong>, 50 mm wide by default, with the height following the design’s own proportions. It works the other way
                        round: the stamp comes out exactly the millimetres you type, and the design is scaled to fit
                        inside it with the margin kept and its proportions untouched. The parts sheet follows the
                        size you set, so the base plate and the handle always match.
                    </p>
                </article>
                <article class="glass reveal rounded-2xl p-6" style="--reveal-delay: 330ms">
                    <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">Merge overlaps first</h2>
                    <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                        Alternating fill is the design's own meaning only while its shapes do not cross. Where two
                        filled shapes overlap, even-odd reads the overlap as a hole — which would engrave a crack
                        through the middle of what should be one solid piece. The tool checks for that and says so;
                        the fix is a union in your design app.
                    </p>
                </article>
            </div>

            <div class="glass reveal mt-5 rounded-2xl p-6">
                <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">The handle</h2>
                <p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                    An engraved face is not a stamp until you can press it. The
                    <strong class="text-slate-300">Handle &amp; parts</strong> tab under the canvas previews the sheet
                    that goes with it — cut lines in red, the glue positions in engraving green — and the
                    <strong class="text-slate-300">Stamp parts sheet</strong> button in the toolbar writes it. Cut it
                    from plywood or acrylic; the engraved face itself wants rubber.
                </p>
                <p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                    A laser makes a three-dimensional grip the only way it can: in layers. So the
                    <strong class="text-slate-300">sheet thickness</strong> decides how many pieces a 20 mm grip
                    takes — seven of 3 mm plywood, three of 6 mm acrylic — and every number is then yours to change:
                    layers, diameter, bar length, upright height.
                </p>
                <dl class="mt-4 grid gap-4 text-sm leading-relaxed sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-4">
                    <div>
                        <dt class="font-semibold text-slate-200">Discs</dt>
                        <dd class="mt-1 text-slate-400">
                            A plain stack of ⌀ 15 mm circles glued into a knob. The quickest to cut and the easiest
                            to glue straight, because every layer is the same.
                        </dd>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <dt class="font-semibold text-slate-200">Knob</dt>
                        <dd class="mt-1 text-slate-400">
                            The same stack with the diameters graded from the base up, so it domes into something
                            that fits the pad of a thumb rather than a cylinder that digs into it.
                        </dd>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <dt class="font-semibold text-slate-200">Bar</dt>
                        <dd class="mt-1 text-slate-400">
                            A rounded bar across the back, the shape an office stamp has. It starts at 70 % of the
                            stamp's width — a long plate that one knob presses unevenly comes down flat.
                        </dd>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <dt class="font-semibold text-slate-200">Arch</dt>
                        <dd class="mt-1 text-slate-400">
                            Two uprights glued on edge with a grip bar across their tops, so your fingers go
                            <em>under</em> the handle. The only one whose height is its own rather than a multiple of
                            the sheet.
                        </dd>
                    </div>
                </dl>
                <p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                    The <strong class="text-slate-300">base plate</strong> comes with all of them: the exact size of
                    the stamp's outer contour — the whole plate, not just the artwork on it — so the face glues on
                    edge for edge, with the handle's footprint engraved on it to glue against. Pick
                    <strong class="text-slate-300">None</strong> and that is the only part you get, for a face going
                    into a mount you already own.
                </p>
            </div>

            <div class="glass reveal mt-5 rounded-2xl p-6">
                <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">What each format does with it</h2>
                <dl class="mt-3 grid gap-4 text-sm leading-relaxed sm:grid-cols-3">
                    <div>
                        <dt class="font-semibold text-slate-200">SVG</dt>
                        <dd class="mt-1 text-slate-400">
                            One path, <code class="text-slate-300">fill-rule="evenodd"</code>, in surface-engraving
                            blue — the inversion exactly as previewed, at true size in millimetres.
                        </dd>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <dt class="font-semibold text-slate-200">DXF</dt>
                        <dd class="mt-1 text-slate-400">
                            DXF has no fills, so every ring goes out as a closed contour in the engraving colour and
                            the alternation is left to your laser software — which is how nested closed contours get
                            filled there anyway. Switch that colour to a fill/scan operation.
                        </dd>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <dt class="font-semibold text-slate-200">Falcon Design Space</dt>
                        <dd class="mt-1 text-slate-400">
                            One surface-engraving shape holding the plate and every ring. An .fds shape is a
                            QPainterPath, whose default rule is odd-even, so it opens ready to run.
                        </dd>
                    </div>
                </dl>
            </div>

            <p class="reveal mt-5 text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-500">
                A note on stamp material: this tool gives you the geometry, not the depth. Laser-engraved rubber
                needs enough material removed that the background cannot touch the paper — usually a fill at high
                power with several passes, or a defocused pass — and a shoulder angle only a 3D/greyscale engrave
                can produce. Cut a test tile before committing a full sheet.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>
</ToolPage>
